    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "In-situ stress analyses using wellbore failures detected in ten wells were conducted for the Yufutsu fractured basement reservoir, Japan. The result shows that the in-situ stress field varies systematically in the reservoir, and indicates that there are correlations between the local stress states and productivities of the wells.\r\n\r\nWe carry out 3D geomechanical modeling to reproduce the spatial variation of observed stress states in the reservoir by focusing on activities of pre-existing faults. The faults are divided into two sets. One set is north-south striking and forms a horst structure. The other set is east-northeast striking transverse to the horst structure. Consequently, it is revealed that the transverse faults are more active, subject to the present stress state, compared with the activity of the faults forming the horst structure. The estimated orientation of active faults is consistent with observed trends of micro seismic clouds during massive hydraulic injection experiments. This consistency supports the validity of the geomechanical models.\r\n\r\nHigh potential for secondary activation of fractures, which is caused by slip on the active faults, is distributed in limited areas such as the tips of faults, or where multiple faults interact. This localization may cause a heterogeneous fracture system in the reservoir. Furthermore, according to relative positions between the wells and the active faults, two types of conceptual models of enhanced conduits for fluid-flow are proposed to explain the high performance of wells. One is directly related to the secondary activation mechanism. The other is in the areas unlikely to occur the secondary activation, but is created by evolving architecture of fractures along active faults.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
